\section{Why normalising and resolving are separate}{
Which company a string names does not change with time. Who owns that company
does. Keeping them apart is what lets one acquisition be one row rather than a
rewrite of every alias beneath it, and it is why \code{maude_resolve_owner()}
requires \code{as_of} while \code{maude_normalize_manufacturer()} takes no date at all.

In the pipeline above, \code{company} counts every St. Jude report as Abbott, which is what a question
about today's market wants. \code{at_time} counts a 2010 report as St. Jude, which
is what a question about who was shipping the device then wants. Neither is a
default, because picking one silently would answer a question the caller did
not ask.
}

\section{Searching for a company that has acquired others}{
Normalising happens after the reports are in hand, so it cannot help with the
reports a query never asked for. A filter on \code{"Abbott"} does not reach the
St. Jude reports of 2016, and nothing about the result says so.
\code{maude_search_terms()} is the direction that fixes it:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{abbott <- maude_search_terms("Abbott")
maude_query(manufacturer_name = abbott$term, limit = 1000)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

With \code{as_of}, the set is restricted to what the company owned on that date;
without it, every name the company's entities have ever filed under is
returned, which is what a retrospective query wants.
}

\section{Why the modality needs the brand}{
Product code does not determine ablation modality. \code{OAE} covers cryoablation
and radiofrequency alike, so a rule that resolves it from the product code and
falls back to radiofrequency will put cryoballoon, pulsed-field, and laser
devices into a radiofrequency arm. Deriving the modality from the brand is what
separates them, and a matched device that delivers no energy, such as a
mapping catheter or a sheath, carries \code{NA} rather than being swept into an arm.
}

